Thinking: We need to answer user question: "How do I load a .pmtiles file as a source in MapLibre GL JS?" Use the skill content. Provide step-by-step instructions, code example, mention installing pmtiles library, registering protocol, using url property, ensure CORS, etc. Also note MapLibre version changes for addProtocol. Provide example for vector and raster. Also mention hosting requirements. Provide troubleshooting tips. Should be concise but thorough.

Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for loading a **single‑file `.pmtiles` archive** into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm – any will do
npm i pmtiles
# (or: yarn add pmtiles)
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler that makes MapLibre understand URLs that point at a `.pmtiles` file.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the scheme “pmtiles”
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why “once”?**  
> `addProtocol` registers a global handler. Registering it inside a React component’s render loop or inside a `new maplibregl.Map()` call will create duplicate handlers and can leak memory. Put the code in a module that runs at app start (e.g. `src/pmtiles-setup.js`) or in a top‑level `useEffect(() => { … }, [])`.

> **MapLibre v4+ note** – the `pmtilesProtocol.tile` shim automatically adapts to the new promise‑based signature (`async (params, abortController) => ({data})`). You don’t have to write any wrapper yourself.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### 3‑a. Vector tiles (most common)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // The name you’ll reference in layers
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // “pmtiles://” tells the protocol to treat the URL as a PMTiles file
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
        // No need to list `tiles: [...]` – the protocol reads the archive header
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw the “water” layer that lives inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water-fill',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the PMTiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },

      // Example: draw roads
      {
        id: 'road-line',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### 3‑b. Raster imagery

```js
// Add a raster source that points at a raster‑PMTiles file
map.addSource('satellite', {
  type: 'raster',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery/sat.pmtiles',
  tileSize: 256               // optional – defaults to 256
});

// Then a raster layer
map.addLayer({
  id: 'satellite',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'satellite',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.9 }
});
```

### 3‑c. Raster‑DEM (terrain)

```js
map.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation/terrain.pmtiles',
  encoding: 'terrarium'   // or "mapbox"
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

