## 4️⃣ Hosting requirements for the `.pmtiles` file

| Requirement | What to check |
|-------------|---------------|
| **HTTP Range support** | The server must honor `Range: bytes=` headers. All S3‑compatible stores (AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, Wasabi, etc.) do this out of the box. |
| **CORS** | The response must include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) **and** `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. Otherwise the browser blocks the range request. |
| **Cache‑Control** | Because the archive is immutable, set a long max‑age, e.g. `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000`. CDNs will cache the range responses as well. |
| **File size limits** | GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc. often cap static files at 100 MiB. For larger archives use S3/R2 or split the dataset into several PMTiles files. |

---

## 5️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank vector tiles** (no features) | The style used `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` instead of `url:`. | Use `url:` – the protocol reads the archive header and supplies the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`. |
| **404 / 403** | CORS or missing `Range` support on the host. | Verify the host returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and proper `Access-Control-Allow-*` headers. |
| **MapLibre logs “Tile request failed” with status 0** | The protocol handler wasn’t registered before the map was created. | Move the `addProtocol` call to a module that runs once at app start. |
| **Error “Tile data must be an object with a `data` property”** | Using an old version of `pmtiles` (pre‑3.0) with MapLibre v4+. | Upgrade `pmtiles` (`npm i pmtiles@latest`). The built‑in shim now returns `{data}` correctly. |
| **Over‑zooming past the archive’s max zoom shows empty tiles** | You referenced the archive with `tiles:` (template) instead of `url:`. | Switch to `url:`; the protocol will automatically clamp zoom to the archive’s max and let MapLibre over‑zoom gracefully. |

