## 4. Wire the archive into a MapLibre map

### 4.1 Install the PMTiles library

```bash
npm install pmtiles maplibre-gl
# or yarn add pmtiles maplibre-gl
```

### 4.2 Register the protocol (once, before any map is created)

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create a protocol instance (v3+ works with all MapLibre versions)
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register the handler globally
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

// Optional cleanup (if you ever need to remove it)
// export const removePmtilesProtocol = () => maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

> **Important:** The handler must be added **before** you construct the first `new maplibregl.Map()`. The registration is global, so you only need to do it once for the whole page or app.

### 4.3 Build a style that points at the archive

You can either embed a full style JSON in your code or load an external style file. The key part is the source definition:

```js
// src/map.js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import './pmtiles-setup'; // ensures protocol registration

// The URL to the file on GitHub Pages
const pmtilesUrl = 'pmtiles://https://<user>.github.io/<repo>/tiles/mytiles.pmtiles';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map', // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // One source per PMTiles file
      mytiles: {
        type: 'vector',          // or 'raster' / 'raster-dem' if you stored those
        url: pmtilesUrl          // note: **use `url:`**, not `tiles:`
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw water polygons from the layer named "water" inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'mytiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      // Example: draw roads from the layer named "road"
      {
        id: 'road',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'mytiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1.5
        }
      }
      // Add any other layers you need – they all share the same source.
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

#### Why `url:` matters

- `url:` tells the PMTiles protocol to read the **archive header** (which contains `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, bounds, etc.) and automatically generate a TileJSON for MapLibre.
- If you use `tiles: ['pmtiles://.../{z}/{x}/{y}']` you bypass that header, forcing MapLibre to assume a default `maxzoom: 22`. The client will then request zoom levels that don’t exist in the archive, resulting in blank tiles. So always use `url:` for PMTiles.

### 4.4 Adding raster‑dem (optional)

If your PMTiles file contains elevation tiles (Terrarium or Mapbox encoding), add a raster‑dem source and enable terrain:

```js
// Add to the `sources` object
elevation: {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://<user>.github.io/<repo>/tiles/elevation.pmtiles',
  encoding: 'terrarium'   // or "mapbox"
},
// Then enable terrain in the map options
terrain: {
  source: 'elevation',
  exaggeration: 1.5
}
```

